    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, anchored by **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (72.88) and **Andy Pages** (71.05). **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** benefits from a favorable matchup against Rea, and while he faces a slight dynamic platoon drag, his raw base projection and high-leverage lineup spot make him the safest anchor in the infield. **Andy Pages** presents a more intriguing profile today; he draws a soft matchup against Rogers and receives a strategic bump due to the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, which significantly improves his probability of securing a stolen base\u2014a critical factor for our internal scoring model given the premium on speed. **Kyle Tucker** (70.02) also remains a top-tier play, as wind conditions out at his venue provide the necessary support to mitigate the unfavorable matchup against a lefty.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today was keeping **Miguel Vargas** (61.26) on the bench. He posted a robust efficiency score driven by a favorable park and platoon outlook, but he ultimately fell behind **Nolan Schanuel** (62.14) and **Josh Jung** (61.51) due to slightly thinner opportunities for high-impact production in the current order hierarchy. While the bench remains strong, we have opted to prioritize the daily efficiency floor of the starters over the fringe upside of **Miguel Vargas**. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, particularly with **Cole Carrigg** (50.00), who faces an elite arm in Skenes. Despite the park shift to a hitter-friendly environment, the talent gap between the pitcher and hitter makes this a marginal play; **Cole Carrigg** stays in only to ensure we maintain roster depth against a high-strikeout arm. We are also monitoring the matchup for **Gunnar Henderson** (65.41), who must contend with Yamamoto. While the wind provides a positive variance, the sheer skill of the opposing pitcher makes this a \"Strong\" start that requires close attention if the game environment shifts.",
